prompt:
Why do all my buttons alert 3?

```js
for (var i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
  const b = document.createElement('button');
  b.onclick = () => alert(i);
  document.body.append(b);
}
```

text:
`var` is scoped to the whole function (or the global scope here), not to each loop iteration. So there is only one `i`, and all three click handlers share it. The loop finishes before you click anything, and by then `i` is 3 (the value that made `i < 3` false). Each handler reads that same variable when it runs, so each alerts 3.

The fix is to use `let`, which creates a new `i` for each iteration:

```js
for (let i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
  const b = document.createElement('button');
  b.onclick = () => alert(i);
  document.body.append(b);
}
```

Now the buttons alert 0, 1 and 2, because each arrow function captures the `i` from its own iteration.

If you can't use `let`, you can get the same effect by passing `i` into a function, so each handler gets its own copy:

```js
for (var i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
  const b = document.createElement('button');
  b.onclick = ((n) => () => alert(n))(i);
  document.body.append(b);
}
```
